WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.440
Thanks to the memorable characters they brought
to life on the big screen and on our TVs,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.720
these actors will never really leave us.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.560
In fact, they'll always be there exactly the
way we remember them.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.580
They'll never be more than a few clicks away.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.660
Former child actor Jon Paul Steuer, who played
a young Klingon on Star Trek: The Next Generation,

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.980
died on January 1st at the age of 33.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.560
Steuer began professionally acting at the
age of four, and was the first actor to play

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.150
Alexander, the mostly-Klingon son of Worf
on Star Trek: The Next Generation.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.190
He portrayed the character in one episode,
"Reunion," and other actors went on to play

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.340
the role at various ages.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.560
"Why can't I stay with you?"

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.480
"You deserve a home."

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.080
He's perhaps best known for playing Brett
Butler's eldest son on the sitcom Grace Under

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.000
Fire from 1993 until leaving the show in 1996.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.060
"You're not sitting up here making plans to
be dysfunctional, are you?"

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.640
"What's dysfunctional?"

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.750
He later said he quit acting in part because
of Butler's erratic behavior and controversies

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.120
surrounding the series.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.680
His death was announced on the Facebook page
of the rock band P.R.O.B.L.E.M.S, in which

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.620
Steuer sang under the name Jonny P. Jewels
for about a year.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.580
"Watch out for Salami Sam!"

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:22.100
Former child actor Joseph Wayne Miller made
his breakthrough in the 1995 comedy Heavyweights.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.500
According to TMZ, he died in his sleep on
January 9th at the age of 36.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.860
Miller played the character of Salami Sam
in Heavyweights, which was co-written and

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.420
produced by Judd Apatow.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.060
In a statement to Page Six, Apatow said:

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.140
"We could not have loved spending time with
him more.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.330
He made everyone around him so happy.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.740
What a terrible loss."

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.020
"Hey, what's up?

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.300
I'm Verne Troyer."

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.860
Verne Troyer stood at two feet eight inches,
and was born with achondroplasia dwarfism,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.720
a condition that causes cartilage to not properly
convert to bones, often resulting in undersized

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.540
arms and legs.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.580
In 2012, he told Jonathan Ross:

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:07.570
"You're born kind of proportioned, and, you
know, you look pretty much normal except for

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:08.080
being small."

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.760
He grew up in an Amish community in Michigan
and achieved fame when he debuted opposite

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.700
Mike Myers in 1999's Austin Powers: The Spy
Who Shagged Me.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.780
Troyer had more than 50 acting credits in
movies and on television, including roles

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:26.739
as Griphook in Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's
Stone and Coach Punch Cherkov in The Love

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:27.240
Guru.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.120
Verne Troyer died on April 21st at the age
of 49.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.299
According to People, his death was noted by
the coroner as possibly being a suicide via

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.400
alcohol poisoning.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.160
An announcement on Troyer's Facebook page
read:

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.580
"It is with great sadness and incredibly heavy
hearts to write that Verne passed away today.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.060
Verne was an extremely caring individual.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.200
He wanted to make everyone smile, be happy,
and laugh."

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:57.660
Actress Pamela Gidley passed away at the age
of 52 on April 16th, dying peacefully in her

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.600
New Hampshire home.

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:04.499
An actress since 1986, Gidley's debut role
was in the skateboarding movie Thrashin',

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.989
in which she starred alongside childhood friend
Sherilyn Fenn and Josh Brolin.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.320
"He says they pay a thousand dollars to the
winner of some race?"

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.100
"It's no little race.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:14.920
It's a downend."

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:20.560
Early in her career, the model-turned actress
played an android in the cult sci-fi comedy

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.940
movie Cherry 2000.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.240
She went on to have a notable role in the
1992 Twin Peaks prequel film Fire Walk With

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:27.740
Me.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:34.079
In 1992, Gidley had a regular role on the
buddy cop series Angel Street on CBS, starring

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:37.540
opposite Robin Givens as a Chicago homicide
detective.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.660
Her last credited role was in Twin Peaks:
The Missing Pieces, a long-lost compilation

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.220
of extended and deleted scenes from Twin Peaks:
Fire Walk With Me.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.939
Perhaps best known for playing Lois Lane in
the Superman film series, actress Margot Kidder

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.840
died on May 13th, 2018 at her home in Montana.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.760
She was 69 years old.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.180
Kidder moved to Los Angeles shortly after
graduating high school in order to pursue

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:02.680
acting.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:08.060
"In the late 60s, the whole idea was to get
wildly out of control and see how far you

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.720
could go before you crashed."

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:16.320
In 1972, she moved to New York City and scored
a role in the Brian De Palma movie Sisters,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.520
a critically-acclaimed psychological thriller.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.540
But her onscreen coupling with Superman, played
by Christopher Reeve, really put her on the

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.040
map.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.880
"Should we get on with the interview?"

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.740
"Yes, let's, let's get on with..."

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.180
The romance between the two characters played
out across four films between 1978 and 1987.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:44.630
Kidder was known for a variety of other roles,
with performances in the horror movies Black

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.160
Christmas and The Amityville Horror.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.950
The actress suffered from mental illness all
her life, and a 1996 bipolar episode led to

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.120
a high-profile disappearance.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.680
She opened up about her struggle to Canada
AM in 2003:

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.190
"I attempted suicide several times, yeah.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.180
I just always assumed I'd never get to be
this old, I'm a grandmother now."

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.500
As she told The Idaho Press-Tribune in 2017,
she remained actively involved in causes she

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.920
believed in, protesting against war and the
excesses of the energy industry.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.430
"I've mostly been doing political activism…

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.520
I was at Standing Rock in a tent for four
months."

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.620
Anthony Bourdain was a writer, presenter,
producer and performer who helped shape how

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.620
we see international food culture.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.240
He died on June 8th in France, at the age
of 61.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.520
Bourdain rose to prominence in 2000 on the
back of his bestselling book Kitchen Confidential:

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.140
Adventures in the Culinary Underbelly.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.240
By the time it was published, he'd already
gathered more than 20 years of experience

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.360
in the kitchen, and his unapologetic attitude
and confessional style helped endear him to

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.360
a large audience.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.200
"Detoxing from what?

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.300
From what, a heroin addict?

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.040
I mean, go home and do some more heroin."

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:59.540
The book was adapted into a television series
in 2005, starring Bradley Cooper as a "bad

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.620
boy chef" based on Bourdain himself.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.600
That image helped make Bourdain a charismatic
presence on the international food scene.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.280
He started appearing on television as the
host of Food Network's A Cook's Tour, an adaptation

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.620
of Bourdain's second book.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:17.820
Bourdain continued his career as a host with
the series No Reservations, The Layover, and

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.640
Anthony Bourdain: Parts Unknown.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:25.020
His notoriety also led to winking cameo appearances
in film and television, with Bourdain appearing

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.030
as himself in the 2015 film The Big Short.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:29.160
"It's not old fish.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.480
It's a whole new thing.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:31.980
That is a CDO."

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.420
He was decorated with awards from the entertainment
industry for his work, earning five Emmys

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:38.720
and a Peabody Award.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.260
Bourdain tragically died of an apparent suicide
by hanging, his body discovered by a friend

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.590
and colleague in his France hotel room on
the morning of June 8th.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:51.980
During his life, the chef made no secret of
his struggles with depression and history

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.890
of self-destructive behavior, remarking in
one 2016 interview: "I should've died in my

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:57.380
20s."

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:03.240
Burt Reynolds was a movie star known for his
displays of genial charm and unique charisma

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.790
over the course of his storied 50-year career.

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.680
He died on September 6th at the age of 82.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:14.720
Born on February 11th, 1936, Reynolds began
his career on Broadway and television shows.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.060
He went on to leave his mark on American cinema
through appearances in a number of successful

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:23.340
films, including Deliverance, The Longest
Yard, and Smokey and the Bandit.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:32.160
"We have a big chance, a big chance, to make
a run for some big bucks: 80,000 of 'em."

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:38.640
His outsized persona and sex symbol status
made him a fixture of pop culture in the 1970s

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:45.970
and 80s, and he enjoyed wild success between
1978 and 1982 as the industry's top-grossing

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:46.840
movie star.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:51.700
Despite his natural talents as a box office
draw, Reynolds spent much of his career seeking

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:56.220
validation as an actor, rarely earning much
critical respect for his performances.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.920
In his 2015 memoir, he claimed he didn't start
challenging himself as a performer until he

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.250
was older, being more interested in leading
an exciting life than cultivating a body of

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:05.740
work.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:11.140
For his work in Boogie Nights, he was nominated
for an Academy Award for best supporting actor.

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.710
He lost the Oscar but won a Golden Globe.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:18.120
He also won two Golden Globes and an Emmy
for his work on the comedy series Evening

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:18.620
Shade.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:20.960
"We got this far somehow.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.220
You can believe somehow."

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:27.460
October is a month that's been associated
with The Walking Dead for many years now,

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:31.040
but in 2018, it brought unwelcome news for
fans of the show.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:36.140
Just one day before the ninth season premiere
on October 7th, actor Scott Wilson passed

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:37.960
away after a battle with cancer.

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:43.840
The actor, who portrayed farmer Hershel Greene
between 2011 and 2014, was 76.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:49.140
Wilson was a lead cast member in seasons three
and four, and he became a popular figure among

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:49.960
the cast and crew.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.740
His passing was announced on The Walking Dead's
official Twitter account:

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.320
"Our thoughts are with his family and friends.

00:08:56.329 --> 00:08:57.470
Rest in paradise, Scott.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:57.960
We love you!"

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.260
In an official statement, AMC said that Hershel
was always at "the emotional core" of The

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:07.731
Walking Dead and that he continues to "inform
our characters' choices to this day," long

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:08.760
after his death.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:13.960
If you're from northeastern America you'll
probably recognize the late James Karen's

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.430
face, even if you never knew his name until
now.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:21.120
The character actor will be forever remembered
as "The Pathmark Man" because of an advertising

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.920
campaign for Pathmark supermarkets that lasted
almost two decades.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.580
"So come to Pathmark.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.060
Come to Number One!

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:32.620
Twenty-four hours a day, for value and savings
over all."

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:37.279
Karen actually lived in Los Angeles, miles
away from the nearest Pathmark store.

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:40.200
He passed away at the age of 94 in his L.A.
home.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.720
The Pennsylvania native also popped up in
hundreds of Hollywood movies over the years,

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.200
including Return of the Living Dead and 1982's
Poltergeist.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:53.519
Good buddy George Clooney claims Karen once
tricked him into writing his obituary prematurely,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:54.200
saying:

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.819
"He just wanted to know what everybody thought
about him while he was still around.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.480
He got a bunch of people to do it."

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:05.260
Emma Chambers, the British actress known for
the romantic comedy Notting Hill, died February

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:07.319
21st at the age of 53.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.790
According to the BBC, she died of natural
causes.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.620
"This is one of those key moments in life
when it's possible to be really genuinely

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:20.280
cool, and I, I'm going to fail just a hundred
percent."

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.480
Her breakthrough came in 1994 when she starred
in the BBC miniseries based on Charles Dickens'

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.440
Martin Chuzzlewit.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:32.790
She also played village church verger Alice
Tinker on the popular BBC comedy The Vicar

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:36.760
of Dibley, appearing in all 20 episodes of
the show through 2007.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.639
American audiences probably remember her best
as Honey, the eccentric younger sister of

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.840
Hugh Grant's character in 1999's Notting Hill.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:50.600
She also played Helen Yardley on the TV series
How Do You Want Me? from 1998 to 1999.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:56.200
Stan Lee became such a beloved figure - not
only with comics fans, but among everyone

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.720
in general - with his down to earth style
and folky mannerisms.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.889
We all knew he wasn’t in great health, but
still in November 2018, when his own epic

00:11:04.889 --> 00:11:08.800
arc came to an end at the age of 95, we were
all crushed.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:16.060
Born in 1922, Lee's first gig at the age of
17 was as a gofer at Timely Publications.

00:11:16.069 --> 00:11:20.380
Comic books were far from mainstream at the
time, but the success of Superman over at

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:23.240
DC Comics, gave owner Martin Goodman hope.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:28.480
He tasked Lee with writing a story for Captain
America #3, and within the span of two years

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.100
the youngster had become editor-in-chief at
Timely, later known as Marvel Comics.

00:11:33.449 --> 00:11:37.900
From his cluttered office in Manhattan, Lee
helped create numerous classic comic book

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:42.870
characters and superhero teams, from Spider-Man
and Black Panther to the Fantastic Four and,

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.240
of course, the Avengers.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:49.720
Lee famously made short-but-sweet cameo appearances
in Marvel movies, with his personal favorite

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:53.980
being the time Thor got him blind drunk in
Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.480
"Eggshellsior."

00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:59.100
He told Playboy in 2014,

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.580
"Keep moving forward, and if it's time to
go, it's time.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:03.700
[...] Nothing lasts forever."

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:09.020
It's difficult to overstate just how much
the Hollywood owes to Penny Marshall, who

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:14.700
died on December 17 at the age of 75 due to
complications from diabetes.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:19.160
Her status as a legend would've been cemented
if she'd only been known for being one half

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:21.589
of the popular TV duo Laverne &amp; Shirley.

00:12:21.589 --> 00:12:26.920
But that was only the beginning, as her impact
on television and film went much, much further.

00:12:27.089 --> 00:12:31.680
She became a true trailblazer for women in
show business when she directed the Tom Hanks

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.260
star-making vehicle Big, which was the first
female-directed film to gross more than $100

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:37.760
million.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:42.440
And a few years later, she directed A League
of Their Own, which the Library of Congress

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.860
selected for preservation in the National
Film Registry in 2012 for its cultural significance.

00:12:49.089 --> 00:12:52.980
Sadly, these aren't the only actors who left
us in 2018.

